e the classical determinant and relate to r-equilibrium problems and hypergraph invariants. For r = 2 and r = 3, such maps are known to be nontrivial, but the situation for higher r is unresolved.\n\nDetermining nontriviality for r ≥ 4 is central to understanding whether these maps provide meaningful invariants across all dimensions d and to extending their applications in combinatorics and mechanics.\n\n### References\n\nIf r ≥ 4 it is not known if the map det Sr is nontrivial or not (some particular cases were discussed in the appendix of [4]).\n\n— The $r$-equilibrium Problem  (2405.10407 - Staic, 2024) in Section 2 (Preliminaries)\n\n### Related Problems\n\n1.\n\nFormulation of a mixed pair-and-triple interaction model and its determinant-like invariant  Same Paper\n\n2.\n\nUniqueness of det S2 beyond the known low-dimensional cases  Same Paper\n\n3.\n\nUniqueness of det S3 for d ≥ 3  Same Paper\n\n4.\n\nExistence and uniqueness conjecture for det S_r  Same Paper\n\n5.\n\nGeneral explicit expressions for higher discriminants that vanish on low-rank bundles  2503.15365\n\n6.\n\nBases and determinants for incidence matroids of l–r set inclusion  2510.19563\n\n7.\n\nNonzero trace for permutation weights and degree of Tr RSK_{m,n,d}  2410.23009\n\n8.\n\nBases and determinants in the discrete Grassmanian incidence matrix  2510.19563\n\n9.\n\nOpen status of structural characterization for k-wise correlations when k ≥ 4  2602.22183\n\nContent\n\nStatement Background References Related Problems--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFlat chain conjecture in Euclidean space (general m) (https://www.emergentmind.com/open-problems/flat-chain-conjecture-euclidean-space-general-m)\nciteturn7search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; — Another simple proof of the 1-dimensional flat chain conjecture  (2603.29683 - Bouafia et al., 31 Mar 2026) in Section 1 (Foreword) ... Nonzero trace for permutation weights and degree of Tr RSK_{m,n,d}  2410.23009\n\n# Flat chain conjecture in Euclidean space (general m)\n\nDetermine whether, in Euclidean space R^d, Ambrosio–Kirchheim metric m-currents correspond to classical Federer–Fleming flat m-chains of finite mass for general m, thereby establishing the full flat chain conjecture beyond the cases m = 1 and m = d.\n\n### Background\n\nThe paper studies the relationship between Ambrosio–Kirchheim metric currents and classical de Rham/Federer–Fleming currents in Euclidean space. The central open problem, known as the flat chain conjecture, asserts an equivalence between metric m-currents and classical flat m-chains of finite mass.\n\nThis work provides another short proof of the conjecture in the one-dimensional case m = 1. The zero-codimensional case m = d is known by De Philippis and Rindler. The general case for 2 ≤ m ≤ d−1 is not settled here and remains conjectural in the presentation.\n\n### References\n\nIn Euclidean space $R^{d$,} metric $m$-currents are conjectured \\S11 to correspond to classical flat $m$-chains of finite mass; this is known as the flat chain conjecture.\n\n— Another simple proof of the 1-dimensional flat chain conjecture  (2603.29683 - Bouafia et al., 31 Mar 2026) in Section 1 (Foreword)\n\n### Related Problems\n\n1.\n\nEuclidean flat chain conjecture  2508.08017\n\n2.\n\nNonzero trace for permutation weights and degree of Tr RSK_{m,n,d}  2410.23009\n\n3.\n\nSierpinski conjecture (m = 5)  2404.01307\n\n4.\n\nExtension of the Alberti–Marchese structure theorem beyond finite-dimensional Euclidean spaces  2508.08017\n\n5.\n\nDemailly’s Conjecture (general m) on Waldschmidt constants  2401.11297\n\n6.\n\nDuchi’s noncommutative AM–GM conjecture (matrix products)  2511.02864\n\n7.\n\nDemailly’s Conjecture for m = 2  2401.11297\n\n8.\n\nStatus of the Local Mizohata–Takeuchi Conjecture  2502.06137\n\n9.\n\nCAT(0) conjecture for the stratified Euclidean metric on polynomial space  2410.03047\n\n10.\n\nAsymptotics of p-capacity for m-dimensional balls (m<d)  2509.21253\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://lacim.uqam.ca/fr/seminaire.html)\nciteturn7search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; With Thomas Lam we completed the proof of a bijection, proposed in my 2021 paper, between the integer points of the positive tropical Grassmannian $\\mathrm{Trop}^+G(k,n)$, modulo lineality, and noncrossing tableaux. ...   * 2026-10-09 ...   * 2026-09-25 ... While the latter does not settle an open question, it strikes us as important to bring to attention, given the interest in finding a solution to Fox’s conjecture. ... For certain families of Hessenberg functions, we show how the functions counting matrices with prescribed Jordan form in the corresponding nilpotent Hessenberg Lie algebras can be used to study the values of irreducible characters on these Kazhdan–Lusztig basis elements. ... This conjecture was proven nearly 20 years later by Caputo, Liggett and Richthammer (JAMS, 2010). ... Here is a purely combinatorial problem that arose in causal set theory. ... Kaveh Mousavand, Okinawa Institute of Science and Technology ... The analysis of the random billiard trajectory relies on new finite Markov chains called stoned exclusion processes, which are variants of certain interacting particle systems.\n\n### Séminaires précédents\n\n  * 2026-10-23\n\nNick Early, Institute for Advanced Study\nHarder–Narasimhan polytopes and the positive tropical Grassmannian\n\nRésumé\n\nWith Thomas Lam we completed the proof of a bijection, proposed in my 2021 paper, between the integer points of the positive tropical Grassmannian $\\mathrm{Trop}^+G(k,n)$, modulo lineality, and noncrossing tableaux. These are also the integer points of the noncrossing fan of Santos, Stump and Welker, but for $3 \\le k \\le n-3$ the bijection is not an isomorphism of fans. In a positive parametrization, the positive tropical Grassmannian corresponds to the normal fan of the product of the Plücker coordinates. In 2021 I proposed replacing some of the Plücker coordinates by other polynomials, so that the normal fan becomes the noncrossing fan. The new polynomials give conjectured solutions to binary u-equations, which define a generalized worldsheet associahedron for CEGM amplitudes. Arkani-Hamed, Frost, Plamondon, Salvatori and Thomas have since attached u-equations to every algebra of finite representation type, with boundaries governed by Harder–Narasimhan polytopes, the convex hulls of the dimension vectors of subrepresentations. Parking functions refine the Ehrhart polynomial in the “magic basis” using a “lucky cars” statistic, this implies a positivity phenomenon in that basis and implies the $h^\\ast$-polynomial is real-rooted. And the $h^\\ast$-polynomial can also be characterized by parking functions by an ascent statistic.\n\n  * 2026-09-25\n\nAlex Vidinas, Cornell University\nA dimer view on Fox's trapezoidal conjecture\n\nRésumé\n\nAbstract: Fox’s conjecture (1962) states that the sequence of absolute values of the coefficients of the Alexander polynomial of alternating links is trapezoidal. While the conjecture remains open in general, a number of special cases have been settled, some, quite recently:  Fox’s conjecture was shown to hold for special alternating links by Hafner, Mészáros, and Vidinas (2023) and for certain diagrammatic Murasugi sums of special alternating links by Azarpendar,  Juhász, and Kálmán (2024).  Any alternating link can be written as a diagrammatic Murasugi sum of special alternating links; thus, if one could remove the certain adjective in the previous result, it would settle Fox’s conjecture. In this paper, we give an alternative proof of  Azarpendar, Juhász, and Kálmán’s aforementioned result via a dimer model for the Alexander polynomial. In doing so, we not only obtain a significantly shorter proof of  Azarpendar,  Juhász, and Kálmán’s result than the original, but we also obtain several theorems of independent interest regarding the Alexander polynomial, which are readily visible from the dimer point of view. Moreover,  we explain, backed with computational evidence,  why our result is only for certain diagrammatic Murasugi sums of special alternating links as opposed to all.  While the latter does not settle an open question, it strikes us as important to bring to attention, given the interest in finding a solution to Fox’s conjecture. This talk is based on joint work with Karola Mészáros and Melissa Sherman-Bennett.\n\n  * 2025-11-21\n\nDoron Puder, Institute for Advanced Study\nAldous-type spectral gaps in Unitary groups\n\nRésumé\n\nAround 1992, Aldous made the following bold conjecture. Let A be any set of transpositions in the symmetric group Sym(N). Then the spectral gap of the Cayley graph Cay(Sym(N),A) is identical to that of a relatively tiny N-vertex graph defined by A. So even though the spectrum of the Cayley graph contains N! eigenvalues, the largest non-trivial one always comes from a tiny pool of N of them. This conjecture was proven nearly 20 years later by Caputo, Liggett and Richthammer (JAMS, 2010). In a joint work with Gil Alon, driven by the conviction that such a stunning phenomenon cannot possibly be isolated, we found a probable parallel of this phenomenon in the unitary group U(N). We have a concrete conjecture supported by simulations, and we prove it in several non-trivial special cases. As it turns out, the corresponding spectrum in the case of U(N) contains the one in Sym(N). Moreover, the critical part of the spectrum in U(N) coincides with the spectrum of an interesting discrete process we call the “multiset process”. A combinatorial proof of the (strong) semibrick conjecture for biserial algebras\n\nRésumé\n\nLet A be a finite-dimensional algebra over an algebraically closed field k. A finite-dimensional (left) A-module is called a brick (or Schur representation) if its endomorphism algebra is isomorphic to k. A set S of bricks over A is said to be a semibrick if every pair of distinct bricks in S are Hom-orthogonal. Bricks (respectively, semibricks) can be viewed as a conceptual generalization of simple (respectively, semisimple) modules, and they have important applications in representation theory of algebras. In 2021, Enomoto posed a conjecture, now known as the Semibrick Conjecture: an algebra A admits infinitely many non-isomorphic bricks if and only if A admits an infinite semibrick– i.e., there exists an infinite set of pairwise Hom-orthogonal bricks.\n\nIn our recent work (arXiv:2407.20877), we made important progress on a series of open conjectures. Specifically, we treated the Semibrick Conjecture as well as a conjecture I first proposed in 2019, now referred to as the Second Brick-Brauer-Thrall (2nd bBT) Conjecture. While both the 2nd bBT and Semibrick conjectures remain open in full generality, we verify them for some new families. In fact, we prove that the Semibrick Conjecture follows from the 2nd bBT Conjecture. For biserial algebras, we reduce the setting to an explicit list of gentle algebras, where we use the combinatorics of strings and bands to resolve the conjectures. This talk is based on earlier work from my PhD studies and a series of joint work with Charles Paquette.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThin Solid Films 401 (2001) 94–101 (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=559f6e969e7e594ba45e5eb77a0fb798824bd650&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn7search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Thin Solid Films 401 (2001) 94–101 ... A great demand exists to explore the exceptional properties of known materials in this family for many significant applications and to push the limits of cur- rently available materials. ... The existence of ternary Si–C–N 3 4 compounds, however, has been vaguely proposed, but is difficult to verify with certainty because of the tendency of SiC and Si N to undergo nanophase sepa- 3 4 rat